  "body": "Parent: [#6024](https://github.com/developing-today/code/issues/6024) (licensing gate for the external corpus). Research source: prior-art doc `~/workspace/scratch/atlas-ingestion-brief-2026-11-04/prior-art-research.md` (2026-11-04). Follow-up 34 (Cluster 6).\n\n## Task\n\nPackage the five questions the GitHub ToS text does **not** settle (from the gate research, Cluster 6 of the prior-art doc) for one lawyer review session. Per the gate's answered-provisional framing on #6024: the external design proceeds on the zone rules and Zones 2–3 full text stays **personal-search-only** meanwhile; this packet is the path to lifting that restriction deliberately. The metadata layer does not wait on it.\n\n1. Does D.6's inbound=outbound reach issue/PR/discussion *comment text*, or only contributed files/patches?\n2. Is a private, single-user, non-commercial search index (embeddings + stored text + snippet display to the owner only) over others' public comments fair use in the US? Does storing *embeddings* change the analysis vs storing text?\n3. What changes if the corpus is ever queried by anyone else (agents acting for Drew? family? a future ID-graph publication)? Where exactly is the redistribution line — snippets in answers? full text in an export?\n4. EU/UK exposure: do text-and-data-mining exceptions apply to a US-based personal user, and do GitHub's terms or repo-level signals constitute an opt-out?\n5. What deletion/takedown process should the corpus implement to stay on the right side of D.5's license-termination logic (the deletion-propagation design, sibling under #6024, is the engineering half)?\n\n## Done looks like\n\nA packet doc (questions + the ToS excerpts + the zone policy) ready to hand to a lawyer, linked from #6024 and the policy decision (sibling under #6024).\n\n<!-- agent-note:v1 agent=muse state=filed source=main-chat-2026-11-04-wave2 -->",
